the retail sales figures are devilishly  difficult to predict it's what they they  do tend to get revised quite a lot so so  it's a though the numbers were markedly  worse than the city was anticipating as  I say they are very very hard to uh call  and very very prone to revision the one  the certainty that we did have over  September uh figures was that uh  obviously we had an extra bank holiday  because of the late Queen's funeral and  so uh spending would have taken a hit  anyway but it's really really  interesting when you look at the  components involved in spending food  sales volumes down 3.2 percent on where  they were going into the pandemic so  people are buying less foodstuffs that  reflects uh inflationary pressures I  think you're certainly seeing it in  motor fuel sales which were down by more  than one percent month on month that is  obviously a reflection of higher costs  of petrol and Diesel and then  interestingly you had a big drop off in  non-store sales the bulk of which I  think that includes Street Markets but  predominantly it is accounted for by  online shopping and so there's an  interesting conclusion to be drawn there  that online sales do appear to be under  an element of pressure as well and that  was actually borne out by something that  we heard yesterday from Asos one of the  big online fashion retailers they  produce some pretty disappointing  numbers yesterday so yeah clearly  consumers uh disposable incomes are  under great pressure it's clear that the  bank of England's interest rate Rises  are starting to have an impact and that  is uh of course uh wide retail sales are  falling now on the public sector  finances well these were markedly uh  worse than expected as well as you say  and chiefly it is down to the fact that  government borrowing costs have risen  boring costs alone 7.7 billion pounds  during the month of September the  government has borrowed in the financial  year to date  133 billion pounds that's uh up from the  office of budget responsibilities  forecast which is 127 billion that's  where they thought we would be at this  point in the year and that that is done  largely down to inflation because around  a quarter of the national debt some 28  of it interest payments are linked to  the rate of inflation and accordingly  with inflation uh Rising sharply so too  have uh interest payments on that  component of the national debt so that's  why you're seeing this big increase in  interest payments as I say 7.7 billion  pounds for September up uh 2.2 billion  pounds on the same month last year and  it's actually the biggest uh year on  year it's the first time we've had a  year-on-year increase in government  borrowing since March 2021 that really  does point to the challenge that the new  prime minister and the new Chancellor  Jeremy Hunt face when in terms of uh  publishing this medium-term fiscal plan  which at the moment is due on the 31st  of October there are one or two people  suggesting that might have to slip in  order for the new prime minister to get  their hands on the the books and have a  look at uh Mr Hunt's projections and see  what they want to do but that would  really have come with a risk because is  the the Focus right now in the market in  the markets is on the need for stability  I've been talking to the economist  Gerard Lyons earlier on today here's  what he told me  debt to GDP in the UK is high but it's  manageable but what happens when debt to  GDP is high it makes the fiscal Outlook  very dependent on your assumptions about  growth and also on the outlook for  interest rates I think the scale of  tightening we're now seeing might be  overdoing it but there's no doubt that  we've got to a situation where stability  has taken center stage and that's  unfortunately some people's eyes taking  too much of a center stage but as  someone looking at from the markets  perspective I'm saying well stability  has to now be the issue that's addressed  now I should probably tell you uh  Kimberly what's been going on on the  markets and reaction to all of this  Sterling has fallen again against the US  dollar it's off by uh just over one  percent right now against the US Dollars  soft by slightly less against the Euro  now that is not certainly a reflection  of the data that we've had out today I  think it's still markets taking in what  we heard yesterday from Ben Broadbent  he's the Deputy Governor at the bank of  England and he made a speech yesterday  in which he indicated that were interest  rates to Peak at five and a quarter  percent which is where the market has  been pricing it he said that would clip  uh some five percent from GDP over the  course of the rate tightening cycle and  he went on to say and this is a really  really crucial intervention from Ben  Broadbent he said that uh the market was  perhaps pricing in a higher Peak uh for  bank rate than uh it uh ought to be and  accordingly you've seen Sterling selling  off accordingly people have think that  the peak interest rate rate Rises will  now not be as high as the market has  been pricing and that's a factor there  also where pointing out that the guilt  Market has been showing a few signs of  distress this morning now obviously  guilt yields have come down very very  sharply since uh quasi quateng was  sacked a week ago and most of his mini  budget torn up yields which are implied  government borrowing costs they've been  going in the right direction so far this  week but we've seen a slight blip higher  in yields this morning and that is  something that again the new Chancellor  will be keeping a closed tab on worth  pointing out though that uh the UK  giveaways are now yielding less they're  lower than the U.S treasury counterparts  and that is really quite an interesting  development because they were at very  elevated levels even compared with US  Treasury yields just a couple of weeks  ago  okay Ian thanks a lot
 

